    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\n1970 Plymouth Satellite Springs Back to Life After 36 Years at a Farm; Too Many Doors?\n12 photos\nPhoto: Rocket Restorations/YouTube\nIn 1965, Plymouth redesigned its intermediate car lineup for the first time. While the Belvedere remained in production, the Savoy was discontinued, while the Fury nameplate was returned to the full-size market. To replace the latter, Plymouth introduced the Satellite.\nA more upscale version of the Belvedere, the original Satellite was also a more performance-oriented Mopar. It came standard with a V8 engine, and it was restricted to two-door body styles (hardtop and convertible). It was Plymouth's range-topping intermediate until 1967, when the company debuted the GTX.\nWith the latter taking over premium muscle car duties, the Satellite was overhauled for the 1968 model year, gaining additional body styles. Much like the cheaper Belvedere, the B-body was also offered in four-door sedan and station wagon iterations. Plymouth retained this lineup until the Satellite was discontinued in 1974.\nAlthough it's overshadowed by the GTX and Road Runner , the Satellite gets a bit of love from classic car collectors, particularly when equipped with the 426-cubic-inch (7.0-liter) Hemi V8. These cars are also quite rare. The 440-cubic-inch (7.2-liter) V8 models are also somewhat sought-after in two-door body configurations.\nOther engines and body styles? Well, these cars are far from desirable, especially if they have four doors. Moreover, most of them are actually rotting away in junkyards and barns, with no hope of getting back on the road. And that's precisely why you need to see this 1970 Satellite.\nFar from surprising for a four-door sedan, this Plymouth was parked decades ago. It retired in 1990 and spent the next few decades at a farm, without a single sip of gasoline. But unlike many of its siblings, it was kept safe from the elements, so it soldiered on without significant rust issues. Its interior was infested by rodents, but the body is in surprisingly good condition after all these years.\nAlso unlike most of its siblings, this Satellite got a second chance at life. YouTube's \"Rocket Restoration\" found it by accident on Facebook Marketplace and decided to send a friend and bid for it at a far auction. He got it for \"a reasonable price,\" brought it back home, and got it running again with help from his buddy at YouTube's \"Dead Dodge Garage.\"\nThe Satellite was born with the 318-cubic-inch (5.2-liter) V8 engine, which was the standard powerplant in 1970. The original owner opted for the three-speed TorqueFlite automatic, which means that this Mopar sports a rather common drivetrain.\nIs it worth reviving to the point it becomes road-worthy? Well, it may become a nice car once that dreadful interior is cleaned up (it sure looks like it smells terrible in there). However, I can't see this Satellite getting a full restoration. It's just not worth it relative to its current market value.\nAnd as our host puts it, some of its body panels fit other more desirable Mopars, and selling them separately would bring more bucks. Unfortunately, this Satellite may end up being dismantled. It's a shame, but that's how the cookie crumbles sometimes.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nPlymouth Satellite classic car 1970 plymouth satellite Plymouth Mopar barn find farm find\nAbout the author: Ciprian Florea\nAsk Ciprian about cars and he'll reveal an obsession with classics and an annoyance with modern design cues. Read his articles and you'll understand why his ideal SUV is the 1969 Chevrolet K5 Blazer. Full profile\nUP NEXT Your Next BMW Will Be Made by AI and This Could Get Very Wrong",
